{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\"**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was closely related to the royal family of Jerusalem through his mother Hodierna who was the sister of Queen Melisende. He was also a cousin of King Baldwin III and Amalric I of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key supporter of the Byzantine Empire and maintained good relations with Emperor Manuel I Komnenos. In 1161 he married Eschiva of Bures a niece of the Prince of Antioch. This marriage strengthened his ties with the northern Crusader states. Raymond III was also involved in the succession disputes in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He supported Amalric I against Miles of Plancy in 1174. After Amalric’s death he helped secure the throne for Baldwin IV the Leper King. Raymond served as regent for the young king from 1174 to 1177 and again from 1184 to 1185.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III is perhaps best known for his role in the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the main Christian commanders who led the army of Jerusalem against Saladin. The Crusader forces were decisively defeated and Raymond was one of the few leaders to escape the disaster. After Hattin he attempted to negotiate with Saladin to secure the safety of Tripoli but was unsuccessful. Raymond III died later that year in 1187. His death marked the end of the direct line of Raymond of Saint-Gilles the founder of the County of Tripoli. The county was later inherited by his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III’s reign was marked by political maneuvering and military challenges. He navigated the complex alliances of the Crusader states while dealing with external threats from Muslim powers. His regency periods were crucial in maintaining stability in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the early reign of Baldwin IV. Despite his failures at Hattin his leadership was pivotal in shaping the history of the Crusader states in the late 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.1741926521062851}, {"token": "Summary", "logprob": -0.8540441989898682}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0010895751183852553}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.27949970960617065}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -9.572047565598041e-05}, {"token": " page", "logprob": -0.4741675853729248}, {"token": " \"", "logprob": -0.20303146541118622}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0001012035645544529}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576272320060525e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.842378934379667e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.002563525689765811}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.965400386136025e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.0009754904895089567}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -5.221230458118953e-05}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.04294806718826294}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.7418097943154862e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -3.0874729418428615e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.34226009249687195}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.10573932528495789}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -8.106198947643861e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009531007381156087}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0006895071710459888}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.2755313036905136e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00016318420239258558}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.000596107158344239}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00017772526189219207}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.